<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alalakh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell Atchana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alalakh is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> city mound at Tell Atchana near Antakya in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amuq-valley">Amuq plain</a>. It was the capital of the kingdom of Mukish, under Yamhad in the 18th century BCE and later under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley</a>'s excavations from 1936 found palaces, temples and archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets, as well as the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrimi">Idrimi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alalakh.jpg" alt="Alalakh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fkitselis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaeological_site_of_Alalakh_(Tell_Atchana).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
